#{[17592186431143 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431143 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431143 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431143 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431143 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431140 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431143 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431143 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431143 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186431128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431143 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cf16abc1-c544-5419-8b7c-43b624566e09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431144 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431144 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94279" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431144 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431144 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8y=0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t)$\n\nNote that $R,{ϕ}_{1},{ϕ}_{2},A$ and $B$ are constants." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431144 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3951f87d-bb0f-5303-830e-d70693674542" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431144 #edb/ref 1074 "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=8\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t)$\n\nTherefore, only statement III could be used to represent  $y(t).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431145 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431145 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94282" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431145 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431145 #edb/ref 1071 "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $8\\,\\text{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431145 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "58a73fa1-b7ae-5890-859a-ca230c9a8f1d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431145 #edb/ref 1074 "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude, and $ϕ$ is the phase. In our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=16\\,⟹\\,\\omega =4.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0)=0.$ Substituing this into the equation gives\n\n$$\nR\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=0\\,⟹\\,ϕ=0.\n$$\n\nSo our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $8\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that ${y}^{′}(0)=8.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4R\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=8$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n8=4R\\,⟹\\,R=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $2\\,\\text{m}.$ The solution for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431146 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431146 #edb/ref 1069 "q-64257" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431146 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431146 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle of mass $5\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=30\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. \n\nIf the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, then which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The governing differential equation is ${y}^{″}+6y=0$\n\nII. The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f=\\frac{3}{2\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}$\n\nIII. The general solution can be expressed as $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t+ϕ),$ where $R$ is the amplitude and $ϕ$ is the phase." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431146 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a1d55e9e-973d-5821-a2d2-76fe96bd1d7d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431146 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's look at each statement.\n\n- The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{30}{5}=6\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\sqrt{6}\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6y=0.\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is true.\n\n- The frequency $f$ (measured in ${s}^{-1}$) is given by\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{1}{T}=\\frac{\\omega }{2\\pi },\n$$\n\nwhere $T$ is the period. So in our case, we have\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nSo the frequency $f=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\nHence, statement II is false.\n\n- The general solution for $y(t)$ can take one of three forms.\n\n1\\. $A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\n2\\. $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n3\\. $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\nWriting $\\omega =\\sqrt{6}$ and ${ϕ}_{1}=ϕ$ in form 3 gives\n\n$$\ny(t)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{6}t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nHence, statement III is false.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
